<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Meng Chang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/919">919</a>–<a href="/years/965">965</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Meng Chang was the second and last emperor of Later Shu, one of the Ten Kingdoms, reigning from 934 to 965. Under his long and peaceful rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> prospered, and his court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a> became a centre of culture and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a>. In 965 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> conquered Later Shu; he was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> and died soon after.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born Meng Renzan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a> in 919, he was the fifth son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meng-zhixiang">Meng Zhixiang</a>, an officer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-cunxu">Li Cunxu</a>, Prince of Jin, by a Lady Li who had been Li Cunxu's concubine. After Li Cunxu, as emperor of Later Tang, destroyed Former Shu in 925, Meng Zhixiang became military governor of Xichuan Circuit at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a>. When his father executed the monitor Li Yan in 927, the boy, his mother and his father's wife were detained for a time at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fengxiang">Fengxiang</a> on their way to join him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meng-zhixiang">Meng Zhixiang</a> declared himself emperor of Later Shu in 934 and, mortally ill that autumn, made Renzan crown prince and regent. Three days after his father's death he took the throne at 15, changing his name to Meng Chang as the will directed. He soon had the general Li Renhan, accused of plotting treason, arrested and put to death with his son, and forced the senior general Li Zhao into retirement.</p><p>His long reign was largely peaceful. The court supported the arts, and the Amidst the Flowers Anthology of lyric poetry was compiled in 940, but the state stagnated militarily and politically. Around the new year 947, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Khitan Liao</a> took the Later Jin capital, He Chongjian surrendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>, Jie and Cheng Prefectures to Later Shu, and Feng Prefecture followed. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-zhou">Later Zhou</a> attacked in 955 to recover them, Meng put to death his attendant Zhao Jizha, who had fled back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a> in panic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 964 he sent secret letters in wax pills to Liu Jun of Northern Han proposing joint strikes on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>; one messenger defected, and the Song emperor took the letters as his justification and sent 60,000 men on two fronts. After his surrender Meng travelled with his family down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> in spring 965, where he was received with ceremony and made Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>, but he died a few days later. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-song">Emperor Taizu of Song</a> named him Prince of Chu; his mother, Empress Dowager Li, stopped eating and died soon after.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Meng Chang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Meng Chang</b> reigned for over thirty years in peace.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-shu">Later Shu</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.87,112.54&amp;year=948" aria-label="Taiyuan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7357 802 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Meng Chang"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7807 1072h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7807 1072h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.87,112.54&amp;year=948">What was here in 948 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
